    "product_id": "714f710b-818e-4f4e-a177-83a5c76b2569",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 130906\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n106 AM PST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Unsettled weather returns to the region today and lasts through \n  Saturday\n\n* Additional precipitation and cooler weather arrives Monday and \n  Tuesday and again in the second half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through next Thursday\n\nThe pattern changes today as a trough and low pressure dip into \nthe region with strong southerly winds building into the mid to \nlate morning with precipitation close to follow the initial wave \nof stronger winds. Wind Advisories are in effect Friday into early\nSaturday with 20 to 30 mph sustained southerly winds and gusts to\n55 mph. The first areas affected will be the eastern Sierra by \nthe late morning. A Winter Storm Warning is in effect for \nelevations above 7,000 feet in the Eastern Sierra for Friday into \nSaturday afternoon. Around a foot of new snow will be possible in \nthe Sierra Crest, and 5 to 10 inches of snow expected between \n7,000 ft and 9,500 ft. Rain chances and winds spread across the \nregion into Friday afternoon. Rain shadowing and a poor moisture \nfeed will prevent much in the way of rainfall in the desert \nvalleys, but still offer some measurable amounts for higher \nelevations. The strongest winds will favor high elevations, and \nwill spread to the lower valleys in the eastern portions of the \nCWA, but the western half wont see the hazardously windy \nconditions. Winds ease through Saturday and rain looks to exit \nthat night, leading to calmer conditions for Sunday.\n\nBack to unsettled weather for Monday as a low pressure builds into \nthe area early that morning and spreads through the day. The better \nfeed of moisture for this system doesn't line up until after the low \npressure center crosses the Sierra, limiting Sierra snow potential, \nbut leading to better rainfall for the rest of the region. Rain \nlooks to become widespread into Monday afternoon for the areas east \nof the Sierra Nevada, but rain rates look to stay light to moderate. \nRainfall totals from Monday into Tuesday look modest (0.10 to 0.25\") \nfor all but Northeast Clark Co, Mohave Co, and Lincoln Co which will \nrange from 0.30 to 1.0\" after showers exit to the east. Wednesday\nwill offer another pause in rain, but another batch of showers \narrives early Thursday and could linger into next Friday.\n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill remain at or below 8 knots, following typical, daily \ndirectional trends through late Friday morning. Southeasterly winds \nwill pick up late Friday morning/early afternoon, gradually veering \naround to the southwest by late afternoon/early evening and \nincreasing to 10 to 15 knot sustained and 20 to 25 knot gusts. VFR \nconditions will prevail with increasing mid-to-high clouds through \nFriday evening.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds across most of the \nregion will remain around 5 to 10 knots and follow typical, daily \ndirectional patterns through Friday morning. Southeast to south \nwinds are expected to increase by late morning through the afternoon \nwith most areas seeing sustained winds 10 to 15 knots with gusts 20 \nto 25 knots. KBIH will see the strongest winds with gusts 30 to 35 \nknots by afternoon. VFR conditions otherwise continue, with \nincreasing mid-to-high clouds through Friday evening.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Gorelow\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
